Yes. Ambiguity can be treated as a designed option space.

Before meaning is fully committed, one utterance can support several plausible interpretations. That gives you room to adapt as context changes, new evidence arrives, or another person reveals how they understood it. In that sense, ambiguity behaves a lot like semantic optionality.

The useful form is:

express loosely → observe interpretation → refine → commit only when needed

That fits your reversible-interaction idea almost perfectly. Early language can stay exploratory; later language can cross an explicit commit membrane when precision or accountability matters.

The interesting thing is that ambiguity can itself become a mechanism inside larger systems. It can enable humor, diplomacy, creative discovery, negotiation, signaling, plausible reinterpretation, gradual disclosure, or testing how different audiences map the same phrase.

But there is an important failure mode: if ambiguity is used to endlessly rewrite prior meaning after outcomes are known, you lose provenance. Then nothing can ever falsify the original claim because every result gets retrofitted into “what we meant.”

So I’d separate:

productive ambiguity = preserves optionality before commitment.
retrospective ambiguity = changes the interpretation after the fact to avoid being wrong.

The first is powerful. The second destroys calibration.

A clean operator would be:

Ambiguity Budget

Preserve multiple interpretations while exploration is useful; progressively collapse them as evidence, coordination needs, or consequences increase.

Or even shorter:

fuzzy upstream, crisp downstream.

That gives you the flexibility without sacrificing the ability to later know what was actually claimed, intended, or decided.